[
 {:name "mcp", :kind "module", :text "Each MCP server is a name of the sandbox, and each tool of the server is a function of that name. The name is the `python_name` of the server in `session[\"env\"][\"mcp\"][\"servers\"]`: the server name in lower case, with `_` for each other character. So `await figma_dev.get_code(node=...)` calls the tool `get-code` of the server `figma-dev`. A tool takes keyword arguments, or one dict. The answer is {server, tool, content, is_error}, and the text of a content block is at content[i][\"text\"]. A tool with an output schema also gives `structured_content`.\n\n`apropos(pattern)` finds the tools and the prompts of each server by name and by description. `doc(<python_name>.<tool>)` prints the description and the input schema of one tool, and `doc(<python_name>)` prints the tools and the prompts of the server. Each connects the server when that is necessary. `await <python_name>()` gives the tools with their input schemas as data.\n\nWrite a tool name as an identifier: each character that an identifier cannot have becomes `_`. `figma_dev[\"get-code\"]` takes the exact name.\n\nA server whose name Python or Vis already has, such as `time`, is a child of `mcp`: its `python_name` is `mcp.time`. A name that the session gave to a value before the server came stays with that value. `mcp.<name>` is the server then, and `mcp[\"<server>\"]` takes the exact name of a server.\n\nPrompts: `doc(<python_name>.prompts.<name>)` prints one prompt, with its text when the prompt needs no argument. `await <python_name>.prompts.<name>(**arguments)` gets its messages, and `await <python_name>.prompts()` lists the prompts.\n\nResources are attachments. `list_attachments()` also lists the resources of the connected servers, each with `source` \"mcp\". `get_attachment`, `read_attachment` and `show_attachment` take the `id` of such a row, or the URI of the resource."}]
